00:33:52.920 --> 00:33:57.760
It's something called Song Parody Rights, and it costs $23,000.

768
00:33:58.360 --> 00:34:03.900
Well, I texted Warren about this, and I was like, how did you guys get assigned to writing this?

769
00:34:04.040 --> 00:34:09.500
And he said, Halstead and I both sang acapella in college, and we were a little nerdy about it.

770
00:34:09.660 --> 00:34:13.080
This was before Pitch Perfect made acapella nerdy cool.

771
00:34:14.680 --> 00:34:16.940
So the writers kind of knew us as that.

772
00:34:17.180 --> 00:34:20.000
We always jumped at the chance to write lyrics for anything.

773
00:34:20.300 --> 00:34:21.560
It was just fun for us.

774
00:34:22.040 --> 00:34:27.380
Lady, both your ex-husband and your current husband sang acapella in college.

775
00:34:27.660 --> 00:34:27.840
I know.

776
00:34:28.360 --> 00:34:30.460
There is a thread to follow there.

777
00:34:30.620 --> 00:34:30.880
I know.

778
00:34:31.020 --> 00:34:33.840
Although, when I was in college, I didn't know anyone that did acapella.

779
00:34:34.500 --> 00:34:35.020
Aca-what?

780
00:34:35.160 --> 00:34:35.980
Aca-what?

781
00:34:36.540 --> 00:34:40.080
Well, lady, thanks to our writer's assistant, Rob Burnett,

782
00:34:40.080 --> 00:34:46.240
I was able to get a hold of the Surfing in the USA version of the Sabre song.

783
00:34:46.500 --> 00:34:52.360
And Cassie had her friend, Yaki Margulies, who is an actor, musician, and big Beach Boys fan,

784
00:34:52.840 --> 00:34:53.820
record it for us.

785
00:34:54.200 --> 00:34:55.120
Here it is.

786
00:34:57.740 --> 00:35:02.640
We'd like to welcome our new boss from Tallahassee Way.

787
00:35:03.720 --> 00:35:08.320
Thanks for making the long trip to Dunder Miffin, PA.

788
00:35:09.660 --> 00:35:14.560
Have a coffee and donut, take off your coat and stay.

789
00:35:15.720 --> 00:35:18.360
Over there, that's Phyllis.

790
00:35:18.560 --> 00:35:20.320
She's working for Sabre.

791
00:35:21.460 --> 00:35:26.400
And back there in the Annex, Kelly and Ryan play.

792
00:35:27.400 --> 00:35:29.520
Kevin, Oscar, and Angela.

793
00:35:31.160 --> 00:35:32.340
Add numbers all day.

794
00:35:40.220 --> 00:35:44.390
Happy to be working for Sabre.

795
00:35:46.190 --> 00:35:50.490
Happy to be working for Sabre.

796
00:36:03.140 --> 00:36:10.240
I mean, that is an amazing Brian Wilson impression.

797
00:36:10.540 --> 00:36:11.700
It's so good.

798
00:36:12.020 --> 00:36:12.460
So.

799
00:36:12.660 --> 00:36:14.100
It's so stinking good.

800
00:36:14.320 --> 00:36:17.260
And Warren and Halstead had to write all those lyrics for that.

801
00:36:17.520 --> 00:36:17.620
I know.

802
00:36:17.760 --> 00:36:19.080
And talk about small world.

803
00:36:19.220 --> 00:36:20.040
I know, Yaki.

804
00:36:20.280 --> 00:36:20.860
I know.

805
00:36:20.860 --> 00:36:27.320
Oh, so he officiates bar and bat mitzvahs, and he officiated a bat mitzvah that you were

806
00:36:27.320 --> 00:36:29.240
at in Ojai, like, very recently.

807
00:36:29.460 --> 00:36:30.440
Three weeks ago.

808
00:36:30.640 --> 00:36:31.540
That's crazy.

809
00:36:31.700 --> 00:36:32.460
He's wonderful.

810
00:36:32.780 --> 00:36:33.900
That was so great.

811
00:36:33.980 --> 00:36:34.960
Yaki, thank you.

812
00:36:35.620 --> 00:36:38.280
Well, Jim is being just adorable new dad.

813
00:36:38.560 --> 00:36:42.700
He's map-quested directions, even though it's just a few minutes away, to their appointment

814
00:36:42.700 --> 00:36:43.880
to look at a daycare.

815
00:36:43.880 --> 00:36:45.980
Pam and Jim are going to tour daycares.

816
00:36:46.140 --> 00:36:47.160
Can I say something?

817
00:36:47.340 --> 00:36:47.660
What?

818
00:36:47.900 --> 00:36:48.740
In this scene.

819
00:36:49.280 --> 00:36:51.260
Yes, Jim is being adorable.

820
00:36:51.520 --> 00:36:59.500
But at five minutes and nine seconds, that fuzzy, eyes-half-closed, frizzy-haired photo

821
00:36:59.500 --> 00:37:02.160
of Pam is very clearly on Jim's desk.

822
00:37:02.440 --> 00:37:03.900
What is that?

823
00:37:04.120 --> 00:37:05.340
What is that?

824
00:37:05.520 --> 00:37:06.280
I don't know.

825
00:37:06.280 --> 00:37:14.300
It is so bizarre to me that this is a photo that has made its way into a frame ever, and

826
00:37:14.300 --> 00:37:16.580
is so prominently on Jim's desk.

827
00:37:16.680 --> 00:37:19.100
Maybe that's the young gal he fell in love with.

828
00:37:19.280 --> 00:37:20.040
That frizzy hair.

829
00:37:20.040 --> 00:37:21.520
She can't open her eyes all the way?

830
00:37:21.820 --> 00:37:21.900
Yeah, I know.

831
00:37:22.060 --> 00:37:23.640
She's constantly mid-blink?

832
00:37:23.800 --> 00:37:26.800
My sister, Billie, rarely has her eyes open in any photo.

833
00:37:27.520 --> 00:37:28.380
I don't know.

834
00:37:28.800 --> 00:37:30.720
I'll let it go, but it's there.

835
00:37:31.220 --> 00:37:36.260
Pam and Jim have a talking head where they explain that they have found a very high

836
00:37:36.260 --> 00:37:37.840
highly rated local daycare.

837
00:37:38.020 --> 00:37:41.860
It's very hard to get into, so they have to go in for an interview.

838
00:37:42.420 --> 00:37:42.900
Yeah.

839
00:37:43.200 --> 00:37:47.720
And I think Jim has his first dad pun where he says, if not, there's always the army,

840
00:37:47.980 --> 00:37:48.900
the infantry.

841
00:37:49.520 --> 00:37:50.980
He's so ready to be a dad.

842
00:37:50.980 --> 00:37:51.760
Dad pun!

843
00:37:52.000 --> 00:37:52.660
Way to go.

844
00:37:53.160 --> 00:37:56.760
Well, there was an alt Jim Pam talking head that was in the shooting draft.

845
00:37:56.760 --> 00:37:58.120
It was a must shoot.

846
00:37:58.640 --> 00:37:59.980
I have to read it to you.

847
00:38:00.100 --> 00:38:00.860
It made me laugh.

848
00:38:02.260 --> 00:38:02.740
Okay.

849
00:38:03.100 --> 00:38:06.180
Pam says, we found a great local daycare.

850
00:38:06.260 --> 00:38:07.620
But it's really hard to get into.

851
00:38:08.060 --> 00:38:11.820
Jim says, ideally, we would have called the moment we conceived.

852
00:38:12.320 --> 00:38:14.540
Pam says, but usually you fall right asleep.

853
00:38:15.200 --> 00:38:15.660
Whoa!

854
00:38:16.820 --> 00:38:17.380
Okay.

855
00:38:17.680 --> 00:38:19.240
Some TMI there.

856
00:38:19.380 --> 00:38:19.780
Yeah.

857
00:38:19.980 --> 00:38:21.020
About Jim and Pam.

858
00:38:21.220 --> 00:38:21.720
I know.

859
00:38:22.400 --> 00:38:23.780
That was a must shoot.

860
00:38:23.780 --> 00:38:24.880
So you shot that.

861
00:38:25.300 --> 00:38:26.600
It exists somewhere.

862
00:38:26.960 --> 00:38:27.300
Wow.

863
00:38:27.660 --> 00:38:31.980
That is so much more than we need to know about their private life, I think.

864
00:38:31.980 --> 00:38:36.400
I have noticed in the shooting draft, whenever we get to the alts, that sometimes they do

865
00:38:36.400 --> 00:38:39.340
peel back a layer to Jim and Pam, and they always back away from it.

866
00:38:39.440 --> 00:38:40.960
They always end up not picking it.

867
00:38:41.120 --> 00:38:46.920
Well, now we're going to go into the conference room, and Gabe is going to play an introduction

868
00:38:46.920 --> 00:38:47.600
video.

869
00:38:48.020 --> 00:38:52.680
It is a video about the new company that has acquired them, Saber.

870
00:38:52.680 --> 00:38:56.220
And it is starring Christian Slater.

871
00:38:56.760 --> 00:38:58.220
As Christian Slater.

872
00:38:58.380 --> 00:38:58.900
I know.

873
00:38:59.080 --> 00:39:00.880
Michael has never been more impressed.

874
00:39:01.120 --> 00:39:04.340
Oh, he, I mean, now he's so excited about this company.

875
00:39:04.480 --> 00:39:04.760
Oh, yeah.

876
00:39:04.940 --> 00:39:06.240
I thought we needed to hear it.

877
00:39:09.100 --> 00:39:11.200
So, you've just been bought by Saber.

878
00:39:11.580 --> 00:39:12.820
You've probably got a lot of questions.

879
00:39:13.740 --> 00:39:15.360
Hi, I'm Christian Slater.

880
00:39:15.740 --> 00:39:17.320
What's it like to work for Saber?

881
00:39:18.000 --> 00:39:19.280
Let's find out together.

882
00:39:20.360 --> 00:39:25.060
Working at Saber means taking on the challenge of the road that rises to meet you.

883
00:39:27.380 --> 00:39:32.660
Saber, respecting the past, but opening the window to the future.

884
00:39:34.380 --> 00:39:36.120
Have you ever tasted a rainbow?

885
00:39:36.920 --> 00:39:39.500
And Saber, you will.

886
00:39:41.910 --> 00:39:44.910
You'll find it easy to embrace the Saber spirit.

887
00:39:45.370 --> 00:39:45.890
Welcome.

888
00:39:46.450 --> 00:39:49.250
We're very excited to go on this journey with you.

889
00:39:50.950 --> 00:39:52.290
Okay, can I say something?

890
00:39:52.850 --> 00:39:57.350
This video opens with the line, so you've just been bought by Saber.

891
00:39:57.470 --> 00:39:58.990
How many companies have been bought?

892
00:39:58.990 --> 00:40:00.150
That's what I want to know.

893
00:40:00.310 --> 00:40:05.850
Like, is this something they made just for this acquisition, or is this like a thing they have?

894
00:40:05.850 --> 00:40:10.550
No, they've bought so many companies and had odd transitions, they had to make the video.

895
00:40:11.210 --> 00:40:12.270
That's my theory.

896
00:40:12.530 --> 00:40:14.730
Well, we got a lot of mail.

897
00:40:14.970 --> 00:40:21.390
A fan mail flurry asking, how did we get Christian Slater to star in this crazy video?

898
00:40:21.970 --> 00:40:27.690
Well, I found an interview that Christian Slater did for Entertainment Weekly about his experience on our show.

899
00:40:27.690 --> 00:40:28.810
Here's what he had to say.

900
00:40:29.270 --> 00:40:35.610
John Krasinski directed the episode, and he and I sort of know each other from different types of events, and he's a really nice dude.

901
00:40:35.810 --> 00:40:43.110
He called my agent and was wondering if I'd be available to do this fantastic PSA on Saber, the company that bought Dunder Mifflin.

902
00:40:43.390 --> 00:40:45.650
And somehow it worked out with the schedule.

903
00:40:45.850 --> 00:40:46.950
It was kind of incredible.

904
00:40:47.750 --> 00:40:48.090
Wow.

905
00:40:48.090 --> 00:40:49.390
So John did it.

906
00:40:49.510 --> 00:40:50.690
John made it happen.

907
00:40:50.870 --> 00:40:51.390
Way to go.

908
00:40:52.110 --> 00:40:56.270
Well, John sent in an audio clip about what it was like to work with Christian Slater.

909
00:40:56.410 --> 00:40:56.950
Here it is.

910
00:40:57.490 --> 00:41:04.590
One of the greatest memories for me in directing this episode was directing that industrial video for Saber.

911
00:41:04.810 --> 00:41:10.790
I mean, just being on set with Christian Slater was a pretty big highlight of my career.

912
00:41:10.790 --> 00:41:19.250
Not only was he one of my favorite actors growing up, but he was the nicest, nicest human being and was such a big fan of the show.

913
00:41:19.270 --> 00:41:26.670
And you could tell he actually just exuded this excitement to be on set, totally understood the joke and knew what he was doing.

914
00:41:26.670 --> 00:41:29.090
And it was just so much fun.

915
00:41:29.610 --> 00:41:39.550
Well, Randy Cordray also shared with us that Christian Slater was a dream to work with, a total professional, cordial, friendly, and engaging with everyone on set.

916
00:41:39.550 --> 00:41:48.690
And Randy said, when we wrapped his work, I witnessed Christian giving up his seat in a courtesy shuttle van so that the entire hair and makeup team could travel together.

917
00:41:48.890 --> 00:41:51.090
Well, that's a Steve Carell move right there.

918
00:41:51.230 --> 00:41:51.710
I know.

919
00:41:51.850 --> 00:41:52.510
So classy.

920
00:41:52.750 --> 00:41:53.170
Mm-hmm.

921
00:41:53.690 --> 00:41:55.830
We did not get to meet Christian Slater.

922
00:41:56.050 --> 00:41:57.350
We didn't get to work with him.

923
00:41:57.390 --> 00:41:58.290
We were so bummed.

924
00:41:58.370 --> 00:41:59.730
We were majorly bummed.

925
00:41:59.790 --> 00:42:02.290
Because everyone was talking about what a nice guy he was.

926
00:42:02.570 --> 00:42:05.390
Well, we got a fan question from Natasha in Wales.

927
00:42:05.770 --> 00:42:08.630
Where was the Christian Slater sequence shot?

928
00:42:08.630 --> 00:42:09.890
Was it close by?

929
00:42:10.290 --> 00:42:13.410
I got a location breakdown from Randy Cordray.

930
00:42:13.610 --> 00:42:14.090
Do it.

931
00:42:14.490 --> 00:42:21.250
The interior shots, including the cafeteria, which, by the way, I asked Randy, was that the IKEA cafeteria?

932
00:42:21.250 --> 00:42:23.550
It really looks like my IKEA cafeteria.

933
00:42:23.850 --> 00:42:24.590
It really does.

934
00:42:24.590 --> 00:42:25.710
We go to the same one.

935
00:42:25.810 --> 00:42:26.950
I was like, where are the meatballs?

936
00:42:27.170 --> 00:42:28.450
That looks like the cafeteria.

937
00:42:28.450 --> 00:42:29.510
He said no.

938
00:42:29.690 --> 00:42:34.510
They were filmed at the Lakeview Corporate Center in Westlake Village, California.

939
00:42:35.070 --> 00:42:40.450
Randy said that they were mostly unoccupied at the time, so we were able to dress them to look like whatever we needed.

940
00:42:40.450 --> 00:42:41.650
We made that cafeteria.

941
00:42:42.030 --> 00:42:45.990
The exterior shots were shot at the Baxter Building.

942
00:42:46.470 --> 00:42:52.990
Randy complimented our locations manager, Kyle Alexander, because he was able to find these two spots right next to one another.

943
00:42:52.990 --> 00:43:05.010
It made it really easy to shoot those sequences in one day, and John shared that when they got to the location, that exterior location, there was a pond in front of the building,

944
00:43:05.010 --> 00:43:11.150
and it had a bunch of ducks in it, like 30 ducks, and John was super excited.

945
00:43:11.390 --> 00:43:19.210
He absolutely loved this detail, so he put Christian Slater's opening shot right in front of the pond with the ducks.

946
00:43:19.210 --> 00:43:30.630
But right before they were about to shoot, one of the location guys got into the pond because they were trying to get the ducks to come just a little bit closer.

947
00:43:30.870 --> 00:43:31.590
To camera?

948
00:43:31.810 --> 00:43:32.110
Yes.

949
00:43:32.510 --> 00:43:33.530
But guess what?

950
00:43:33.990 --> 00:43:35.590
All the ducks flew away.

951
00:43:35.770 --> 00:43:37.570
They were like, get out of our pond, mister.

952
00:43:38.310 --> 00:43:39.490
John was so bummed.

953
00:43:40.050 --> 00:43:47.930
And then our writer, Jen Salata, shared that Greg Daniels pitched that line, have you ever tasted a rainbow?

954
00:43:47.930 --> 00:43:49.630
Oh my gosh, so brilliant.

955
00:43:49.670 --> 00:43:50.530
That was from Greg.

956
00:43:50.930 --> 00:43:53.590
It was even used in the promo that you played.

957
00:43:54.050 --> 00:43:58.010
They did have some alts, like, have you ever tasted a smile?

958
00:43:58.270 --> 00:44:00.930
But ultimately, they kept the rainbow line.

959
00:44:01.190 --> 00:44:06.210
They also experimented with different things that Christian Slater could be doing on that line.

960
00:44:06.450 --> 00:44:11.150
Like, at one point, I guess they had him carrying around a giant basket of fruit.

961
00:44:12.110 --> 00:44:15.050
But they loved him picking up the sandwich.

962
00:44:15.410 --> 00:44:15.650
Yeah.

963
00:44:15.650 --> 00:44:18.770
I mean, they must have had so much fun shooting.

964
00:44:18.890 --> 00:44:21.110
They were just doing these weird, absurd things.

965
00:44:21.330 --> 00:44:23.730
And Christian was playing it so straight the whole time.

966
00:44:23.730 --> 00:44:24.310
It's amazing.

967
00:44:24.870 --> 00:44:33.390
Well, in the Entertainment Weekly article he did, the interviewer asked him if he ad-libbed anything, commenting that the thumbs up at the end was classic.

968
00:44:33.690 --> 00:44:33.910
Oh.

969
00:44:34.310 --> 00:44:35.710
Here was Christian's response.

970
00:44:36.070 --> 00:44:38.410
The thumbs up was a spontaneous moment.

971
00:44:38.410 --> 00:44:42.130
I just kind of thought, what would be the cheesiest thing to do?

972
00:44:42.810 --> 00:44:49.830
John wanted it to really look like Kathy Bates paid for this actor to come in and directed him on what to do behind the scenes.

973
00:44:49.990 --> 00:44:53.550
So I just kept thinking, what would Kathy Bates tell me to do?

974
00:44:53.550 --> 00:44:55.710
I love that motivation.

975
00:44:56.250 --> 00:44:57.910
Of course, Kathy Bates as Joe Bennett.

976
00:44:58.070 --> 00:44:58.610
As Joe Bennett.

977
00:44:58.810 --> 00:44:59.090
Yes, exactly.

978
00:44:59.510 --> 00:45:02.810
Well, we had a fan question from Fiona L.

979
00:45:02.990 --> 00:45:04.250
in Melbourne, Australia.

980
00:45:04.570 --> 00:45:07.370
Our fan questions are very international today.

981
00:45:07.730 --> 00:45:16.090
Well, actually, Fiona has something to tell us, which is that Christian Slater does ads very similar to the Saber introduction video.

982
00:45:16.250 --> 00:45:19.330
They are for HP Wolf Security.

983
00:45:19.630 --> 00:45:24.910
They seem very tongue-in-cheek, especially because he's done this Saber video.

984
00:45:25.170 --> 00:45:26.710
I have never seen this commercial.

985
00:45:27.010 --> 00:45:34.290
I want to say thank you, Fiona, for sending me down the rabbit hole that is the Wolf HP web series.

986
00:45:34.290 --> 00:45:40.310
It was a web series that they launched in 2017 starring Christian Slater.

987
00:45:40.490 --> 00:45:42.170
They're called The Wolf.

988
00:45:42.610 --> 00:45:47.450
And I think Christian Slater is the Wolf because he howls in it.

989
00:45:47.550 --> 00:45:47.830
What?

990
00:45:48.030 --> 00:45:48.610
He howls.

991
00:45:48.790 --> 00:45:53.430
He walks through companies, kind of like he does in the Saber video.

992
00:45:53.590 --> 00:45:56.530
You know, like he walks through the conference room while a meeting is happening.

993
00:45:56.530 --> 00:46:05.450
And all the while he is highlighting how easily it would be for him to hack in to this company's printer or their computer.

994
00:46:05.770 --> 00:46:08.530
He sort of narrates as he's walking through.

995
00:46:08.610 --> 00:46:09.830
I pulled a clip.

996
00:46:11.110 --> 00:46:13.070
Sheep never realizes a wolf around.

997
00:46:15.780 --> 00:46:17.900
And they do exactly what the wolf expects them to do.

998
00:46:18.320 --> 00:46:19.780
They run into each other.

999
00:46:19.960 --> 00:46:20.900
They fall down.

1000
00:46:21.540 --> 00:46:22.780
They become dinner.

1001
00:46:25.490 --> 00:46:26.390
Time to eat.

1002
00:46:29.930 --> 00:46:33.950
They've got no idea I used my phone to hack the OS on that printer over there.

1003
00:46:35.090 --> 00:46:36.090
And why would they?

1004
00:46:36.850 --> 00:46:40.190
Not one printer in this place has built-in malware protection.

1005
00:46:40.770 --> 00:46:44.850
Now, while they're all busy watching a little something I threw up on the control panel...

1006
00:46:45.430 --> 00:46:46.790
Just look at your screen.

1007
00:46:47.970 --> 00:46:51.770
I can intercept all their print jobs until I find the info I'm looking for.

1008
00:46:52.730 --> 00:46:55.650
Ooh, today's somebody's birthday.

1009
00:46:56.190 --> 00:46:56.410
Perfect.

1010
00:46:57.790 --> 00:46:59.510
Really good stuff is upstairs.

1011
00:46:59.930 --> 00:47:06.350
The kind of stuff that could destroy any future this grand financial institution could hope to have.

