It was so funny.

1260
00:57:59.790 --> 00:58:05.030
I guess cutting off the end of Michael saying balls was an idea that John had.

1261
00:58:05.190 --> 00:58:07.630
He came up with it in editing with Dave Rogers.

1262
00:58:07.830 --> 00:58:09.990
And I love their take on this.

1263
00:58:10.130 --> 00:58:14.090
So what they were saying was that obviously Michael completed his sentence.

1264
00:58:14.650 --> 00:58:22.950
But they imagined that when the documentary aired, that the documentarians would have left in all of the footage.

1265
00:58:23.130 --> 00:58:27.330
But the network running the documentary would have to cut it off.

1266
00:58:27.490 --> 00:58:28.050
Right.

1267
00:58:28.050 --> 00:58:30.550
Because of, you know, standards and practices.

1268
00:58:30.550 --> 00:58:38.390
And he said they based that on the amount of times that we had to cut things or change things because of standards and practices.

1269
00:58:38.850 --> 00:58:40.250
Every episode, probably.

1270
00:58:40.490 --> 00:58:41.310
Very realistic.

1271
00:58:41.850 --> 00:58:46.490
Well, listen, Angela, you mentioned the line to fill their water bottles.

1272
00:58:46.690 --> 00:58:50.270
And while Michael is in line, Toby's getting water and it runs out.

1273
00:58:50.390 --> 00:58:53.270
Toby says we kicked it, which cracked me up.

1274
00:58:53.350 --> 00:58:53.830
Me too.

1275
00:58:53.830 --> 00:58:56.670
I wrote it down at 8 minutes, 45 seconds.

1276
00:58:57.210 --> 00:58:59.570
That's what you say when a keg runs out, right?

1277
00:58:59.650 --> 00:59:00.150
I know.

1278
00:59:00.810 --> 00:59:02.870
Well, what does that tell us about Toby?

1279
00:59:03.230 --> 00:59:05.370
Well, I think what does it tell us about Paul?

1280
00:59:05.710 --> 00:59:10.330
Because I've bought a pony keg with Paul and we've kicked it.

1281
00:59:10.550 --> 00:59:16.070
See, I would never use that language because I've never purchased a keg and I've never had a beer out of a keg.

1282
00:59:16.070 --> 00:59:17.030
Oh, man.

1283
00:59:17.410 --> 00:59:18.210
I'm not a beer person.

1284
00:59:18.550 --> 00:59:18.770
Listen.

1285
00:59:19.110 --> 00:59:23.670
Now, I'll do a flavored ale or a cider beer.

1286
00:59:23.950 --> 00:59:29.310
A really, really cold keg and like getting a beer out of a super cold keg.

1287
00:59:29.330 --> 00:59:31.250
It's great, especially like on a hot day.

1288
00:59:31.250 --> 00:59:35.830
Maybe you put one, you know, at second base because you're playing softball with friends.

1289
00:59:35.830 --> 00:59:42.310
And if you make it to second base, you get a beer and you get a little tooted while you're waiting to run into home plate.

1290
00:59:42.730 --> 00:59:45.350
That cold beer out of a keg.

1291
00:59:45.450 --> 00:59:46.290
It's amazing.

1292
00:59:46.290 --> 00:59:48.470
I mean, that sounds miserable to me.

1293
00:59:48.690 --> 00:59:52.570
You drink some beer and then you have to run on your sloshy beer stomach.

1294
00:59:52.910 --> 00:59:57.230
Have you seen like co-ed softball like with a group of friends?

1295
00:59:57.390 --> 00:59:58.830
This is not like real athleticism.

1296
00:59:58.830 --> 01:00:01.590
You have lived part of life that I haven't lived.

1297
01:00:01.990 --> 01:00:03.750
You've had all these experiences.

1298
01:00:04.130 --> 01:00:07.570
And to me, it's like you lived in a foreign land.

1299
01:00:08.090 --> 01:00:08.390
What?

1300
01:00:08.590 --> 01:00:10.730
A foreign land of softball and beer?

1301
01:00:10.970 --> 01:00:11.310
Yeah.

1302
01:00:11.590 --> 01:00:14.990
I have no like frame of reference for that.

1303
01:00:14.990 --> 01:00:17.490
I know you and Lee bond over this stuff all the time.

1304
01:00:17.610 --> 01:00:18.670
Is this a Texas thing?

1305
01:00:18.870 --> 01:00:20.430
I mean, I'm sure they're doing it in Missouri.

1306
01:00:20.490 --> 01:00:21.410
Where was I?

1307
01:00:21.510 --> 01:00:23.190
What was I doing during this time?

1308
01:00:23.190 --> 01:00:26.810
This was just like a hip young person out in the world.

1309
01:00:26.810 --> 01:00:28.430
I've never been a hip young person.

1310
01:00:28.530 --> 01:00:29.150
Well, that's true.

1311
01:00:29.250 --> 01:00:32.150
I've been a 65-year-old woman since the day I was born.

1312
01:00:32.290 --> 01:00:32.770
It's true.

1313
01:00:32.770 --> 01:00:33.850
And I love you for it.

1314
01:00:33.890 --> 01:00:34.510
Thank you, lady.

1315
01:00:34.810 --> 01:00:37.590
As I stare at your big fake flower that's on your lapel.

1316
01:00:37.590 --> 01:00:38.890
I know it's not working.

1317
01:00:38.890 --> 01:00:40.630
I know it's not working.

1318
01:00:44.990 --> 01:00:47.430
This cannot happen on our book launch tour.

1319
01:00:47.870 --> 01:00:48.850
It can't.

1320
01:00:48.970 --> 01:00:49.450
Look at me.

1321
01:00:49.530 --> 01:00:50.330
It's ridiculous.

1322
01:00:50.750 --> 01:00:51.610
You never had to.

1323
01:00:51.610 --> 01:00:53.250
Is water coming out of this flower?

1324
01:00:53.450 --> 01:00:54.190
Squirt, squirt.

1325
01:00:54.330 --> 01:00:55.670
It's borderline clownish.

1326
01:00:55.830 --> 01:00:56.650
It can't happen.

1327
01:00:56.890 --> 01:00:58.690
You never had to prove it to me.

1328
01:00:59.350 --> 01:01:01.150
I've known it from the beginning.

1329
01:01:01.370 --> 01:01:03.590
Well, I needed to prove it to myself.

1330
01:01:04.070 --> 01:01:04.790
All right.

1331
01:01:04.990 --> 01:01:05.950
Where are we?

1332
01:01:06.370 --> 01:01:10.050
Well, Toby has just kicked the water cooler.

1333
01:01:10.050 --> 01:01:11.570
Oh, yes, that's right.

1334
01:01:11.890 --> 01:01:14.110
Well, now Michael's going to confront Gabe.

1335
01:01:14.450 --> 01:01:18.710
He's going to say, listen, I don't mean to seem ungrateful, but I am really struggling

1336
01:01:18.710 --> 01:01:21.770
to embrace some of these changes, such as all of them.

1337
01:01:23.490 --> 01:01:26.090
Gabe says it's kind of above his pay grade.

1338
01:01:26.390 --> 01:01:31.190
He can't really change any of the policies, but he'll see what he can do.

1339
01:01:31.670 --> 01:01:36.870
And now Pam and Jim are going to enter the daycare center.

1340
01:01:36.870 --> 01:01:40.790
All of these scenes were absolutely delicious.

1341
01:01:41.010 --> 01:01:43.090
I loved watching them.

1342
01:01:43.350 --> 01:01:45.390
We had a fan question from Alice R.

1343
01:01:45.490 --> 01:01:45.930
in Italy.

1344
01:01:46.190 --> 01:01:48.490
Where did you shoot the daycare scenes?

1345
01:01:48.630 --> 01:01:49.950
Was it a real daycare?

1346
01:01:50.210 --> 01:01:51.790
Did you make any changes?

1347
01:01:52.730 --> 01:01:54.570
Lady, did you recognize it?

1348
01:01:54.930 --> 01:01:55.490
No.

1349
01:01:56.110 --> 01:01:56.390
Okay.

1350
01:01:56.410 --> 01:01:58.850
Well, it's called Sandbox Daycare.

1351
01:01:58.850 --> 01:01:59.730
We made that up.

1352
01:01:59.930 --> 01:02:02.030
That's not even a real daycare in Scranton.

1353
01:02:02.490 --> 01:02:06.550
We shot this at Peekaboo Playland in Eagle Rock, California.

1354
01:02:06.870 --> 01:02:09.610
Did you not take Isabel to Peekaboo Playland?

1355
01:02:09.690 --> 01:02:11.990
Was that after she was a toddler?

1356
01:02:12.290 --> 01:02:14.810
This was all the rage when my kids were little.

1357
01:02:15.150 --> 01:02:16.490
Eagle Rock's far from me.

1358
01:02:16.690 --> 01:02:17.750
I guess that's true.

1359
01:02:17.790 --> 01:02:23.150
We had tons and tons of Playland-type places right by me, so I never needed to venture

1360
01:02:23.150 --> 01:02:23.710
this far.

1361
01:02:23.930 --> 01:02:26.770
Well, you know, when I did this episode, I didn't have kids.

1362
01:02:26.770 --> 01:02:29.790
But then when I was re-watching it, I was like, it's Peekaboo!

1363
01:02:29.930 --> 01:02:31.270
It's Peekaboo Playland!

1364
01:02:31.270 --> 01:02:34.850
I've spent so many days in this space!

1365
01:02:34.850 --> 01:02:36.790
See, the one by me is the Coop.

1366
01:02:36.930 --> 01:02:38.670
I spent tons of time at the Coop.

1367
01:02:38.770 --> 01:02:39.710
I've been to the Coop.

1368
01:02:39.850 --> 01:02:40.030
Yeah.

1369
01:02:40.590 --> 01:02:40.890
All right.

1370
01:02:41.010 --> 01:02:43.490
So there's a big twirly slide in the middle.

1371
01:02:43.790 --> 01:02:46.670
There's like a treehouse around the curly slide.

1372
01:02:46.670 --> 01:02:48.270
It's the cutest dang thing.

1373
01:02:48.810 --> 01:02:57.490
When we get there, Jim and Pam are totally charmed, except Jim accidentally walks in on

1374
01:02:57.490 --> 01:03:00.630
the daycare director who is using the little kitty toilet.

1375
01:03:01.110 --> 01:03:03.150
Those toilets are so low to the ground.

1376
01:03:03.530 --> 01:03:06.370
Well, they need to be because of the little children.

1377
01:03:06.730 --> 01:03:07.050
I know.

1378
01:03:07.170 --> 01:03:09.010
But seeing a grown man on it really did.

1379
01:03:09.890 --> 01:03:11.070
I was like, oh my God!

1380
01:03:12.990 --> 01:03:16.810
John sent us in an audio clip of what it was like to shoot in the daycare.

1381
01:03:17.050 --> 01:03:18.070
Here's what he had to say.

1382
01:03:18.670 --> 01:03:24.870
My other favorite memory from that episode was the interview at the daycare center.

1383
01:03:25.070 --> 01:03:28.770
Jenna, as we've talked about many times, and I will embarrass you on your own show, I

1384
01:03:28.770 --> 01:03:30.030
apologize, but not really.

1385
01:03:30.650 --> 01:03:34.970
Truly some of my favorite, favorite memories in all of my career were getting to do scenes

1386
01:03:34.970 --> 01:03:35.690
with you, period.

1387
01:03:36.250 --> 01:03:42.750
But especially those scenes where the two of us were sort of stuck in it together, that

1388
01:03:42.750 --> 01:03:45.090
we were both in something that we had to get out of together.

1389
01:03:45.090 --> 01:03:51.850
I remember very specifically your line reading when I came out from seeing Jerry in the bathroom

1390
01:03:52.430 --> 01:03:54.370
and I said, I just saw someone in the bathroom.

1391
01:03:54.670 --> 01:03:57.990
And you very earnestly ask, who was it?

1392
01:03:58.130 --> 01:03:58.850
Who was it?

1393
01:03:58.930 --> 01:04:03.670
And then you quickly digress as your brain realizes, oh, it was definitely the guy that

1394
01:04:03.670 --> 01:04:04.490
we're about to interview.

1395
01:04:04.670 --> 01:04:06.650
And your line reading of who was it?

1396
01:04:06.710 --> 01:04:07.190
Who was it?

1397
01:04:07.350 --> 01:04:09.050
Was it the guy who's going to interview us?

1398
01:04:09.050 --> 01:04:11.430
It was so funny.

1399
01:04:11.650 --> 01:04:16.130
And I don't know that I had a good enough take of my reaction to you.

1400
01:04:16.270 --> 01:04:19.430
In fact, do I physically bail out after you say that to me?

1401
01:04:20.130 --> 01:04:21.970
Man, that really made me laugh.

1402
01:04:22.150 --> 01:04:28.270
I seem to remember going through, I don't know, 10, 12 takes of just not being able to

1403
01:04:28.270 --> 01:04:28.910
keep it together.

1404
01:04:29.210 --> 01:04:32.590
That was a total GiggleFest day for me.

1405
01:04:32.590 --> 01:04:37.330
You were always more professional, but I was in full GiggleFest mode and shout out to my

1406
01:04:37.330 --> 01:04:39.250
friend, Joey Slotnick, who played Jerry.

1407
01:04:39.470 --> 01:04:43.010
I was so excited to get him on the show and he absolutely crushed it.

1408
01:04:43.290 --> 01:04:50.070
That gesture he does in the bathroom where he silently raises his hand, but there is no

1409
01:04:50.070 --> 01:04:53.110
there is nothing said between the two people.

1410
01:04:53.690 --> 01:04:57.130
Just utter shame and embarrassment and guilt.

1411
01:04:57.470 --> 01:04:58.890
He was so great on that show.

1412
01:04:58.890 --> 01:05:03.190
And I just remember yet again, just getting to watch those scenes and not have to give

1413
01:05:03.190 --> 01:05:03.710
any notes.

1414
01:05:04.710 --> 01:05:05.430
All right.

1415
01:05:05.710 --> 01:05:09.610
So I just have to say, I totally remember that.

1416
01:05:09.750 --> 01:05:15.090
Yes, John, you do physically bail out of the scene, but also, you know what?

1417
01:05:15.190 --> 01:05:20.170
I really appreciate him saying that he liked shooting these scenes with me because I think

1418
01:05:20.170 --> 01:05:26.270
there's often a lot written about our romantic chemistry on screen, but we had a really special

1419
01:05:26.270 --> 01:05:31.430
comedic chemistry together, and I feel like this storyline really highlights that.

1420
01:05:31.590 --> 01:05:33.730
We were great partners all around.

1421
01:05:34.050 --> 01:05:34.890
I agree.

1422
01:05:34.890 --> 01:05:39.990
And I loved what John said about when you guys were both in a sticky situation together.

1423
01:05:40.390 --> 01:05:42.190
Yes, I loved it.

1424
01:05:42.570 --> 01:05:47.310
And, you know, John mentioned Jerry, the daycare director, was played by Joey Slotnick.

1425
01:05:47.410 --> 01:05:49.870
He was a really good friend of John's.

1426
01:05:50.010 --> 01:05:53.610
He had also been in John's movie, Brief Interviews with Hideous Men.

1427
01:05:53.610 --> 01:05:57.970
But Joey had actually auditioned for this part through Alison Jones.

1428
01:05:58.270 --> 01:06:03.970
I guess he'd auditioned for other roles on the show, and when this role came up, she

1429
01:06:03.970 --> 01:06:06.050
thought of him, and she knew he was John's friend.

1430
01:06:06.750 --> 01:06:12.870
But you might recognize Joey from the TV shows Alias, Boston Public, The Single Guy, or

1431
01:06:12.870 --> 01:06:13.510
Nip Tuck.

1432
01:06:13.610 --> 01:06:18.750
He was also in the movies Twister and The Secret Life of Walter Mitty, which, by the way, is

1433
01:06:18.750 --> 01:06:21.210
one of my favorite movies directed by Ben Stiller.

1434
01:06:21.210 --> 01:06:24.410
Well, I met Joey years ago when I first came to Los Angeles.

1435
01:06:24.430 --> 01:06:28.910
My friend got me a job just for one season being a stand-in on The Single Guy.

1436
01:06:29.190 --> 01:06:31.090
I didn't know you were a stand-in.

1437
01:06:31.210 --> 01:06:32.850
Yeah, just for one season.

1438
01:06:33.090 --> 01:06:33.350
Still.

1439
01:06:33.670 --> 01:06:34.650
It was so cool.

1440
01:06:34.830 --> 01:06:37.530
What a great way to learn the ins and outs of a four-camera show.

1441
01:06:37.770 --> 01:06:38.170
Yes.

1442
01:06:38.290 --> 01:06:42.050
You know, I did background work for years when I first came out.

1443
01:06:42.170 --> 01:06:42.430
Same.

1444
01:06:42.430 --> 01:06:46.930
A great way to just sit on a set and observe how it works.

1445
01:06:47.110 --> 01:06:52.510
So by the time I finally booked a role and I had a line, I was like, oh, I know the flow.

1446
01:06:52.690 --> 01:06:54.070
Like, I know some of the language.

1447
01:06:54.410 --> 01:06:54.470
Yeah.

1448
01:06:54.470 --> 01:06:55.330
I know this world.

1449
01:06:55.630 --> 01:06:59.410
And in four-camera stand-ins walk through the role while they're lighting.

1450
01:06:59.650 --> 01:07:01.330
They say the lines that go to the mark.

1451
01:07:01.550 --> 01:07:04.990
It was such a great education, and Joey was so nice to everyone.

1452
01:07:04.990 --> 01:07:06.150
That's my memory of him.

1453
01:07:06.150 --> 01:07:11.310
Well, we got some mail asking, why aren't there any kids at this daycare?

1454
01:07:11.650 --> 01:07:12.210
Right.

1455
01:07:12.630 --> 01:07:14.090
Well, there actually were.

1456
01:07:14.410 --> 01:07:20.090
The kids were staged doing a little story time, but they were over to the side.

1457
01:07:20.290 --> 01:07:26.510
So when Pam and Jim arrive, there was like a mini scene of the kids doing story time,

1458
01:07:26.510 --> 01:07:28.730
and Pam and Jim have a little aww moment.

1459
01:07:29.370 --> 01:07:35.370
And then John actually directed me when he was going to walk to the bathroom to go up

1460
01:07:35.370 --> 01:07:40.450
to the little tea set and just be, like, pretending like I was a kid doing tea, and

1461
01:07:40.450 --> 01:07:41.670
I was, like, so charmed.

1462
01:07:42.110 --> 01:07:48.390
And Jen shared that they really liked just following me and John rather than cutting away and showing

1463
01:07:48.390 --> 01:07:48.910
the kids.

1464
01:07:48.910 --> 01:07:54.550
So they actually edited out that story time bit for time because they just liked staying

1465
01:07:54.550 --> 01:07:57.390
with our enthusiasm over the daycare center.

1466
01:07:57.590 --> 01:07:57.930
Oh, yeah.

1467
01:07:58.030 --> 01:07:58.470
I agree.

1468
01:07:58.730 --> 01:08:03.850
You guys are just so delighted to be new parents, and I loved the line when Jim says,

1469
01:08:03.990 --> 01:08:04.610
oh, cubbies.

1470
01:08:04.610 --> 01:08:05.970
I forgot about cubbies.

1471
01:08:06.050 --> 01:08:06.370
Yes.

1472
01:08:06.530 --> 01:08:08.830
Because that's really how it is when you have kids.

1473
01:08:09.050 --> 01:08:11.370
All of these memories come back to you.

1474
01:08:11.470 --> 01:08:12.070
It's true.

1475
01:08:12.410 --> 01:08:17.990
Well, right after this Jim and Pam scene at the daycare would have been the second part

1476
01:08:17.990 --> 01:08:19.330
of this accounting runner.

1477
01:08:19.670 --> 01:08:22.570
The one where Angela's giving fake work to Kevin?

1478
01:08:22.770 --> 01:08:22.970
Yes.

1479
01:08:22.970 --> 01:08:23.710
I love it.

1480
01:08:24.070 --> 01:08:24.750
All right.

1481
01:08:25.030 --> 01:08:28.550
So it reads, interior Vance refrigeration.

1482
01:08:28.730 --> 01:08:32.290
Kevin approaches a desk where an older man is sitting.

1483
01:08:32.650 --> 01:08:34.510
Kevin, excuse me.

1484
01:08:34.630 --> 01:08:35.410
Are you Todd?

1485
01:08:35.690 --> 01:08:37.470
The old man says, yes.

1486
01:08:38.150 --> 01:08:39.710
Kevin says, I'm Kevin.

1487
01:08:39.710 --> 01:08:41.650
I'm an accountant at Dunder Mifflin.

1488
01:08:42.410 --> 01:08:45.150
Todd says, oh, you must know Phyllis.

1489
01:08:45.350 --> 01:08:46.110
She's great.

1490
01:08:46.910 --> 01:08:51.570
Kevin says, Phyllis is, but I didn't come all the way over here to talk about Phyllis.

1491
01:08:51.890 --> 01:08:54.150
Kevin closes the door on the cameras.

1492
01:08:54.850 --> 01:08:55.710
What's happening?

1493
01:08:55.950 --> 01:08:56.650
What's happening?

1494
01:08:57.030 --> 01:08:57.610
Dun, dun, dun.

1495
01:08:57.730 --> 01:08:58.430
Dun, dun, dun.

1496
01:08:59.250 --> 01:08:59.610
Wow.

1497
01:08:59.930 --> 01:09:00.410
I know.

1498
01:09:00.630 --> 01:09:01.710
Part three is coming up.

1499
01:09:02.050 --> 01:09:07.570
Well, this is the part of the show where we're going to see Andy and Erin flirting in the

1500
01:09:07.570 --> 01:09:07.950
kitchen.

1501
01:09:08.390 --> 01:09:09.370
So awkward.

1502
01:09:09.690 --> 01:09:10.430
So awkward.

